Read the S6SY761 touch controller on QUPv3 SE13 and poll its reports.

The OnePlus 8T wires its touch controller to QUPv3 SE13 as an I2C slave at
0x48; the device tree in the vendor boot image has /soc/i2c@a94000 there with
sec-s6sy761@48 underneath, so the SE base comes from the board's own firmware
instead of a guess. This adds a bring-up driver in the style of the USB and
UFS ones: bare MMIO through ml_io_map, no IOKit subclassing, a kernel thread
instead of interrupts, console output only.

It maps the QUPv3_1 wrapper and SE13, programs the GENI core the way the Linux
geni driver does (IO mode, FIFO mode, packing, SCL counters), reads the chip
identity with the Synaptics TCM boot/application info commands, asks for a
known touch report layout and polls for reports. Several hardware facts cannot
be checked without the phone, so they are boot arguments rather than
assumptions: kebab_touch_raw=1 dumps raw SE and TLMM registers and raw report
bytes, and kebab_touch_se=/pins=/reset=/hz= let one run change one assumption
at a time.

Nothing is registered in the IORegistry yet; HID wiring is a later task.

